# The golden expectations are records of bytes, not source files.
#
# Each file under tests/golden/expected/ holds exactly what the tool produced: a
# sample artefact is CRLF because that is what the csv module writes on every
# platform, and a recorded stream is LF because that is what the normalisation
# produces. Both are the point of the comparison.
#
# Git's line-ending conversion cannot tell those apart from an ordinary text file.
# With core.autocrlf on, checking out turns the LF streams into CRLF and a fresh
# clone on another machine reports every stream case as a mismatch; storing a
# sample artefact turns its CRLF into LF, and a fresh clone on Linux compares LF
# against the CRLF the tool actually wrote. Neither failure has anything to do
# with the tool.
#
# So these files are stored and checked out byte for byte, on every platform.
tests/golden/expected/** -text
